{"body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to tell apart two replies that its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" The first reply text is the string consisting of a line with three backticks followed by \"json\", a newline, the line '{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}', a newline, a line with three backticks, a newline, and the line '{\"c\":3}'. The second reply text is the string '{\"c\":3}'. For the first reply, the rule requires the object {\"b\":2}, because it is the last object inside the code fence. For the second reply, the rule requires {\"c\":3}, because there is no fence, so the last top-level object is used. The check, however, returns the same answer for both: it returns the tuple ({\"c\":3}, ()). This is because the fenced candidate '{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}' does not parse as a single JSON object, so the check falls through to the top-level pool and picks the last top-level object, which is {\"c\":3} in both texts. The conjecture would be false if running recover_json_object on the first text returns ({\"b\":2}, ()) or any tuple whose first element is not {\"c\":3}, or if it raises; or if running it on the second text returns anything other than ({\"c\":3}, ())."}